CAT = "zapsiraRole"; SEG = "ROL"
# The security officer's role, conduct and professional standards: the Code of Conduct for
# Security Service Providers (2003) as summarised in sources.md, the Constitution's Bill of Rights
# (ss. 9, 10, 12, 14) and standard guarding practice.
Q = [
("What is the main task of a security officer on a guarding post?",
 "Protect people and property", ["Investigate and prosecute crime", "Enforce traffic law", "Collect debts for the client"],
 "A security officer's job is to protect and safeguard persons and property at the post. Investigation and prosecution are for SAPS and the NPA."),
("Where does a security officer find the specific duties for a particular site?",
 "The post instructions", ["The officer's own judgement", "The client's social media", "The previous shift's gossip"],
 "Post (assignment) instructions set out the duties, patrol routes, access rules and contacts for that site. An officer reads them at the start of every shift."),
("Your relief has not arrived at the end of your shift. What should you do?",
 "Stay and tell the supervisor", ["Lock up and go home", "Leave the keys at the gate", "Ask a cleaner to take over"],
 "A post must never be left unattended. Remain on duty and report to the supervisor or control room so a relief can be arranged."),
("At shift change, what should the outgoing officer hand over to the incoming officer?",
 "Keys, kit and open issues", ["Only the radio and torch", "Nothing, the logbook says all", "Personal contact details"],
 "A proper handover covers keys, equipment, the occurrence book entries and anything still open, such as a faulty gate or an expected delivery."),
("A visitor offers you money to let them in without signing the register. What does this amount to?",
 "A bribe, which you refuse", ["A tip you may keep", "A normal courtesy", "A fee to share with the supervisor"],
 "Accepting money to ignore a procedure is corruption. Refuse, follow the access procedure and report the attempt to your supervisor."),
("Which right in the Bill of Rights means a guard must not treat visitors differently because of race or religion?",
 "Equality", ["Freedom of trade", "Access to courts", "Property"],
 "Section 9 of the Constitution protects equality and bars unfair discrimination on grounds such as race, gender, religion and disability."),
("A shopper shouts at you over a long queue at the entrance. What is the best first response?",
 "Stay calm and explain", ["Shout back to take control", "Ban the shopper at once", "Ignore the shopper completely"],
 "Calm, respectful communication usually lowers tension. Explain the reason for the delay and call a supervisor if the situation escalates."),
("Why must a security officer be neat and in full uniform on duty?",
 "To be identified and trusted", ["To look like a police officer", "To hide personal items", "To avoid paying for clothes"],
 "A clean, complete uniform lets the public and the client recognise the officer and supports a professional image. It must never imitate the police."),
("A colleague arrives for night shift smelling strongly of alcohol. What should you do?",
 "Report it to the supervisor", ["Cover for the colleague", "Share the post quietly", "Send the colleague to sleep in the guardhouse"],
 "An officer under the influence cannot do the job safely. Report it so the supervisor can remove the officer and arrange a relief."),
("A tenant asks you for the alarm code of another tenant's unit. What should you do?",
 "Refuse to give it out", ["Give it if they look honest", "Give it if they know the owner", "Write it on a note for them"],
 "Security information learned on duty is confidential and is shared only with people entitled to it."),
("Which right in the Bill of Rights is most at stake when a guard searches a person's bag?",
 "Privacy", ["Freedom of religion", "Language and culture", "Education"],
 "Section 14 of the Constitution protects the right to privacy, including not having one's person, home or property searched. That is why bag searches need a lawful basis, usually consent as a condition of entry."),
("While on duty a security officer sees a crime being committed off site. What is the usual priority?",
 "Call SAPS and stay at post", ["Chase the suspect alone", "Ignore it, it is off site", "Leave the post to film it"],
 "The officer's duty is to the post. Calling SAPS on 10111 and noting the details helps without leaving the site unprotected."),
("What does the chain of command mean for a security officer?",
 "Reporting via your supervisor", ["Taking orders from any visitor", "Reporting straight to PSIRA", "Choosing your own instructions"],
 "Instructions come from and reports go to the officer's direct supervisor or control room, who passes them up to managers and the client."),
("A client's manager asks you to clean the office kitchen during your shift. What is the best response?",
 "Explain it is not your duty", ["Do it and leave the gate", "Do it but skip the patrol", "Refuse rudely and walk off"],
 "A security officer is posted to protect the site. Politely explain that non-security tasks take you away from your post, and refer the request to your supervisor."),
("Which behaviour shows respect for human dignity when dealing with a suspected shoplifter?",
 "Speak privately and calmly", ["Announce the theft on the intercom", "Photograph the person for social media", "Handcuff them in the walkway"],
 "Section 10 of the Constitution protects dignity. Dealing with a suspect away from the public, calmly and without humiliation, respects it."),
("A security officer notices a broken window in the client's building. What should be done first?",
 "Record and report it", ["Ignore it until morning", "Fix it with your own tools", "Tell the tenants on social media"],
 "Defects that weaken security are noted in the occurrence book and reported to the supervisor or client contact so they can be fixed."),
("A journalist asks a guard on duty for comment about a robbery at the site. What is the right reply?",
 "Refer them to the company", ["Give full details of the robbery", "Share CCTV stills", "Name the suspects you saw"],
 "Guards do not speak to the media about incidents. Enquiries go to the employer or client spokesperson, which also protects the police investigation."),
("Why should a guard avoid using a personal cellphone for long calls while on duty?",
 "It distracts from observation", ["It drains the client's power", "It is always illegal", "It annoys the supervisor only"],
 "Constant phone use takes attention away from the post. Use the radio or phone for duty and keep personal calls for breaks."),
("A deaf visitor arrives at the gate. What is the most professional way to help?",
 "Use writing or gestures", ["Shout louder until understood", "Refuse entry", "Ask the visitor to leave"],
 "Adapting communication, for example by writing on a notepad, serves the visitor fairly and respects equality."),
("The guard at the gate is very friendly with one regular delivery driver and stops checking his load. What is the risk?",
 "Theft can slip through", ["The driver may feel valued", "The register stays neater", "There is no real risk"],
 "Familiarity breeds complacency. Every vehicle is checked to the same standard, however well the driver is known."),
("What is the best way for a guard to stay alert on a long night shift?",
 "Keep moving and vary rounds", ["Sit still in a dark corner", "Nap between the rounds", "Watch videos on a phone"],
 "Moving, varying routines and staying hydrated help an officer stay alert. Sleeping on duty is serious misconduct."),
("An officer finds a wallet in the parking area. What should happen to it?",
 "Record it and hand it in", ["Keep it if nobody asks", "Take the cash for safety", "Give it to the first person who asks"],
 "Found property is recorded in the occurrence book and handed in under the site's lost property procedure, so the owner can claim it with proof."),
("A guard's personal belief differs from a visitor's lifestyle. How should the guard treat the visitor?",
 "Like any other visitor", ["With extra bag searches", "With refusal of entry", "With jokes to lighten the mood"],
 "A security officer must not discriminate unfairly. Every visitor gets the same courteous, professional service."),
("Which of these is a sign of a professional security officer?",
 "Punctual, alert and courteous", ["Loud and intimidating", "Friendly with every visitor's car", "Relaxed about procedures"],
 "Punctuality, alertness and courtesy build trust with the client and the public."),
]
